  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nMain concerns in the medium range are the warm temperatures\nexpected much of next week as well as precipitation chances\nmidweek onward.\n\nA positively-tilted high-amplitude ridge will nose into the\nUpper Midwest and central Plains early next week, allowing for a\nwarm-up of temperatures back to the upper 80s to the mid 90s \nfrom Monday onward in much of the area. The good news is that it\nwill take some time for dew points to increase into the 70s\nacross the area (staying mainly in the 60s through at least\nTuesday), so heat indices will remain in the 80s to low 90s at\nworst through the early week. However, continued northward\ntransport of warm/moist air from the Gulf will gradually allow\ntemperatures to creep into the mid 90s and dew points back to\nthe 70s by the mid to latter portions of next week. As a result,\nheat indices may approach the triple digits again by late week.\n\nHowever, the overall pattern will be changing during the\nWednesday-to-Friday time frame as well. A strong vorticity\nmaximum will progress eastward near or just north of the\nU.S./Canada border on Tuesday, leading to more zonal flow in the\nmid/upper levels in its wake. This should temper the building\nheights in the western/central U.S. to some degree and will also\nbring us into closer proximity to the series of perturbations\nmoving generally eastward in the faster upper flow. One such\nsystem will move into the northern/central Plains on Wednesday\nand Thursday, leading to multiple rounds of convection in the\nbroader region during this period. Models are showing decent\nagreement (especially at this time range) for such convection to\naffect the Missouri Valley Wednesday night and Thursday.\nReinforcing systems are likely later in the week and beyond as\nthe flow becomes subtlely more northwesterly. As a result,\nthough the region will remain on the southern periphery of the\nfaster upper flow, some occasional bouts of convection may\ntemper the warm-up that might otherwise occur in such a pattern.\n\nOf course, this pattern is also one of fairly low\npredictability, owing to its dependence on developing/evolving\nconvection. So the forecast beyond Tuesday becomes one of\nconsiderably lower confidence with time.",
